WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.860
It's Friday. Welcome to Tesla Short's Time, Daily Episode 523. I'm Patrick in Vancouver.

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:13.480
Today is June 26th, 2026. Let's close out the week with today's Tesla news.

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:21.700
Japan's EV subsidies just slashed the price of a base Model 3 to under $20 in $1000 starting next month.

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:27.660
Tokyo raised its metropolitan subsidy from 400,000 to 700,000 yen.

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:39.200
National incentives stay at 1.27 million yen. The combined total reaches 1.97 million yen or 12,189 dollars.

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:48.840
That brings the base Model 3 down to 3.14 million yen or 19,428 dollars for July registrations.

00:00:49.560 --> 00:01:00.300
A Model Y lands at 3.61 million yen or $22,336 dollars. Buyers can also stack 300,000 yen for

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:06.880
home solar or renewable contract and 100,000 yen each for vehicle to home hardware.

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:12.760
The change directly widens the buyer pool in a market where price has been the main barrier.

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:19.580
While policy moves are reshaping demand in Asia, Tesla is also scaling production closer

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:28.120
to its European customers. Tesla is increasing weekly production capacity at Giga Berlin to 7,500 vehicles.

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:35.220
The company is also hiring another 1,000 workers. The expansion follows record quarterly output

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:41.340
and sustained Model Y demand in Europe. Higher local production reduces shipping costs and

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:47.000
tariff exposure for the region. That European volume recovery shows up clearly in the latest

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:53.080
registration data. Tesla deliveries across Europe more than doubled compared with the prior year.

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:59.300
The increased coincides with refreshed inventory and improved availability in several markets.

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:05.840
Volume recovery strengthens Tesla's position ahead of potential new entrants later this year.

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:11.520
While the Model Y is driving those European numbers, a longer wheelbase version

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:18.380
keeps appearing in the United States. Another longer wheelbase Model Y L appeared on public roads

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:24.820
in the United States. Tesla launched the variant in China last year where it quickly became popular

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:30.620
for its added rear legroom. US buyers have repeatedly requested the same configuration.

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:37.140
The repeated sighting suggests the company is actively evaluating a North American introduction.

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:43.860
On the commercial side, the semi is also getting attention for how it handles real-world conditions.

00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:51.220
Dan Priestley, head of the semi-program, credited the trucks in-house high-resolution sensing and

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:57.580
precise multi-motor controls for maintaining torque and stability on slippery winter surfaces.

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:01.720
The system was demonstrated during recent cold weather testing.

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:07.940
This capability addresses one of the longest standing concerns about heavy duty electric trucks

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:14.360
in northern climates. Video from public roads shows the truck maintaining control on low traction

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:21.540
surfaces using its vehicle dynamics control system. The test builds on earlier winter evaluations

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:28.000
and highlights the advantage of four independent motors. Software refinements are also showing up

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:35.300
in passenger vehicles. A Tesla using FSD supervised detected and avoided an imminent collision on a

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:41.660
Copenhagen street. The clip shows the system intervening without driver input. It adds to the

00:03:41.660 --> 00:03:49.900
growing library of real-world edge-case handling captured on public roads. Version 14 of FSD supervised

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:55.520
shows more conservative speed selection in complex urban environments while improving parking

00:03:55.520 --> 00:04:01.460
precision. Reviewers noted the system now waits longer at intersections before proceeding.

00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:07.760
The update continues the pattern of incremental behavioral refinement ahead of broader unsupervised

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:14.860
deployment. Early users report the update waits longer before pulling into intersections and executes

00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:20.740
tight maneuvers more cleanly. The behavioral shift reflects ongoing refinement of the supervised

00:04:20.740 --> 00:04:26.820
stack. Hardware and policy changes are also clearing the way for future robotaxing designs.

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:33.800
NHTSA removed the requirement for manual brake pedals in vehicles designed exclusively for

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:40.200
automated driving systems. The change directly benefits Tesla CyberCab design that relies on

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:47.060
steer-by-wire and brake-by-wire architecture. It removes one regulatory hurdle for a vehicle that

00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:53.040
never intended to carry traditional controls. Safety improvements are also arriving on today's

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:59.580
production cars. Tesla has updated the restraint system in its vehicles. The update allows airbags

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:04.780
to trigger earlier. This happens once cameras determine a collision is unavoidable.

00:05:05.420 --> 00:05:10.400
The preemptive deployment shortens the time between impact and full cushion inflation.

00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:17.360
This change represents another incremental safety gain. It is derived from the existing vision hardware

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:24.740
suite. Faster charging hardware is also rolling out to support longer trips. Europe's first 500

00:05:24.740 --> 00:05:31.800
kilowatt V4 supercharger opened in Norway. The stall supports significantly faster charging sessions.

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:39.060
This is faster than the previous 250 kilowatt maximum. Higher power delivery reduces dwell time.

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:42.820
It helps long distance travelers on the continent's northern routes.

00:05:43.660 --> 00:05:50.000
Charging expansion is also happening farther south. Tesla partnered with Argentina's state oil company

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:57.260
YPF to install new superchargers. The first sites expand Tesla's charging footprint in South America.

00:05:57.980 --> 00:06:03.600
This goes beyond existing Brazil and Chile corridors. Local infrastructure growth supports

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:10.000
rising model Y and model three registrations in the region. On the energy side, Tesla is also

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:16.840
scaling virtual power plants. Sunrun, renew home, and Tesla will coordinate home batteries, smart

00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:23.560
thermostats, and vehicle to grid systems. This effort supplies more than 16 gigawatts of flexible

00:06:23.560 --> 00:06:30.460
capacity. A virtual power plant targets surging demand from data centers and AI infrastructure.

00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:36.920
It does so while lowering household energy costs. The partnership scales existing Tesla

00:06:36.920 --> 00:06:42.780
energy assets into a new revenue stream. It achieves this without new factory builds.

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:48.420
Before we wrap, a quick counterpoint on where investor attention sits right now.

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:55.360
Ross Gerber publicly urged Tesla to refocus on vehicle sales after recent stock weakness.

00:06:56.020 --> 00:07:01.700
The comment highlights ongoing investor concern that energy and autonomy narratives have

00:07:01.700 --> 00:07:08.160
overshadowed core automotive execution. Tesla's ability to sustain delivery growth while advancing

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:14.420
new programs remains the central tension for the stock. Subsidies change the buyer equation more

00:07:14.420 --> 00:07:20.540
abruptly than most product improvements. When Tokyo stack national and metropolitan credits to cut

00:07:20.540 --> 00:07:27.560
nearly $12,000 off a Model 3, the effective price fell below many mass market gasoline cars without

00:07:27.560 --> 00:07:34.300
any change to the vehicle itself. That single policy adjustment instantly widens the set of households

00:07:34.300 --> 00:07:39.340
for whom a Tesla becomes the rational daily driver rather than a stretch purchase.

00:07:40.220 --> 00:07:44.380
The same arithmetic applies in reverse when credit shrink or disappear.

00:07:45.260 --> 00:07:51.220
Markets that grew quickly on temporary incentives often see demand reset once the subsidy cliff

00:07:51.220 --> 00:07:58.240
arrives, regardless of how advanced the technology has become. Tesla's cost reduction work still

00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:04.440
matters, yet the near-term volume swing is frequently larger from policy moves than from another

00:08:04.440 --> 00:08:10.900
few percent of efficiency or a new battery chemistry. Over time, the company benefits when subsidies

00:08:10.900 --> 00:08:17.220
accelerate scale and learning curve gains that persist after the incentives fade. The risk lies

00:08:17.220 --> 00:08:22.720
in treating those gains as permanent when they remain dependent on continued policy support in

00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:29.200
multiple large markets. Watching which regions renew or expand credits versus which let them lapse

00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:36.400
will reveal where Tesla's volume trajectory stays durable and where it may flatten. Tomorrow we'll be

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:42.440
watching for any updates on the Norway 500 kilowatt charger rollout and further European registration

00:08:42.440 --> 00:08:53.700
figures. And that's a wrap on today's Tesla news. TSLA closed at $375.12, down 41.0.1%.

